WEBVTT

00:00:00.00 --> 00:00:04.26
This is journey eleven of Hewn, the software factory: find a project.

00:00:04.26 --> 00:00:07.16
Once Hewn is building several products at once,

00:00:07.16 --> 00:00:11.49
Owners and Viewers need one place to see them all: what each is doing,

00:00:11.49 --> 00:00:15.50
how far along it is, what it has cost, and which needs attention.

00:00:15.50 --> 00:00:19.83
This video walks through how it was built: the rows, the live updates,

00:00:19.83 --> 00:00:24.15
the page and its filter, the empty states, performance, and the tests.

00:00:25.30 --> 00:00:30.94
Projects lists every project with its name, current stage, status, percent complete,

00:00:30.94 --> 00:00:34.03
spend to date and last activity, newest first.

00:00:34.03 --> 00:00:37.73
A status filter narrows the list and survives a reload.

00:00:37.73 --> 00:00:40.21
Rows update in place within a second.

00:00:40.21 --> 00:00:44.85
With no projects, Owners are offered New product and Viewers are not.

00:00:44.85 --> 00:00:50.49
And the page has to load fast on a phone, with read APIs that stay quick under load.

00:00:51.64 --> 00:00:52.78
Here is the flow.

00:00:52.78 --> 00:00:57.21
The page puts the filter in the query string, loads the list once,

00:00:57.21 --> 00:01:00.23
and then joins the projects group on the hub,

00:01:00.23 --> 00:01:02.84
so each changed row arrives on its own.

00:01:03.99 --> 00:01:07.89
Each row is built on request by the project summary reader.

00:01:07.89 --> 00:01:13.30
It reads the projects with their directions, the leased runs for the current step,

00:01:13.30 --> 00:01:18.65
the increments for stage ten, and each project's latest stored event for its last

00:01:18.65 --> 00:01:19.25
activity.

00:01:19.25 --> 00:01:24.27
The row describes the current direction: the active one, or else the latest.

00:01:24.27 --> 00:01:27.24
Its stage is the first one not yet succeeded,

00:01:27.24 --> 00:01:31.93
and percent complete is the share of its planned stages that succeeded,

00:01:31.93 --> 00:01:35.69
with stage ten's merged increments counted as a fraction.

00:01:36.04 --> 00:01:40.08
The list handler sorts those rows newest activity first.

00:01:40.08 --> 00:01:45.28
There is no separate summary table to keep in step: with fifty projects,

00:01:45.28 --> 00:01:48.39
reading on request answers in milliseconds.

00:01:48.39 --> 00:01:50.70
ADR sixteen records that choice.

00:01:51.85 --> 00:01:55.07
For live updates, the hub gained a projects group.

00:01:55.07 --> 00:01:59.33
After events are stored and broadcast to each project's own group,

00:01:59.33 --> 00:02:04.69
the publisher reads every changed project's row and sends it to the projects group.

00:02:04.69 --> 00:02:09.41
Log lines and decisions never change a row, so they don't trigger a read.

00:02:09.41 --> 00:02:13.86
A brand new project arrives the same way, so the page simply adds it.

00:02:15.01 --> 00:02:20.32
In the page, a live row replaces the project's old one, or is added if it's new,

00:02:20.32 --> 00:02:22.25
and the list is sorted again.

00:02:22.25 --> 00:02:26.43
The filter is read from the query string, so a reload keeps it,

00:02:26.43 --> 00:02:30.02
and each tab shows how many projects have that status.

00:02:30.02 --> 00:02:35.60
After a reconnect, the page loads the whole list again, in case it missed something.

00:02:36.75 --> 00:02:41.98
The requirement is a largest contentful paint of two and a half seconds or less on

00:02:41.98 --> 00:02:45.94
Lighthouse's mobile profile, a mid-tier phone on throttled 4G,

00:02:45.94 --> 00:02:49.97
with no more than five hundred kilobytes of initial JavaScript.

00:02:49.97 --> 00:02:53.41
The first measurement was about two point six seconds,

00:02:53.41 --> 00:02:56.61
because each request waited for the one before it.

00:02:56.61 --> 00:03:01.27
Angular preloads only ten files, and the build had eleven initial chunks,

00:03:01.27 --> 00:03:03.50
so one was found a round trip late.

00:03:03.50 --> 00:03:07.59
Start-up fetched the user, then the config, one after the other.

00:03:07.59 --> 00:03:10.97
And the list itself waited until the page was routed.

00:03:10.97 --> 00:03:14.42
Now the build merges its chunks into one initial file,

00:03:14.42 --> 00:03:17.87
and start-up fetches the user and the config together.

00:03:17.87 --> 00:03:22.53
When the app opens on Projects, the list is requested at the same moment,

00:03:22.53 --> 00:03:25.53
and the page takes that response when it loads.

00:03:25.88 --> 00:03:30.98
Lighthouse now measures a largest contentful paint of one point four four seconds

00:03:30.98 --> 00:03:36.21
with a hundred and forty six projects, and one hundred and twenty five kilobytes of

00:03:36.21 --> 00:03:37.41
initial JavaScript.

00:03:37.41 --> 00:03:42.39
The load test makes sure there are fifty projects and twenty connected workers,

00:03:42.39 --> 00:03:45.91
then sends a thousand reads from twenty clients at once.

00:03:45.91 --> 00:03:49.25
The ninety fifth percentile is thirteen milliseconds,

00:03:49.25 --> 00:03:51.33
against a limit of three hundred.

00:03:52.48 --> 00:03:56.47
The backend tests came first, and failed: there was no stage,

00:03:56.47 --> 00:04:00.07
percent or activity in the list, and no projects group.

00:04:00.07 --> 00:04:04.45
Now four tests cover the columns and their order, percent complete,

00:04:04.45 --> 00:04:07.73
a blocked project's note, and a row arriving live.

00:04:07.73 --> 00:04:12.04
The frontend gained unit tests for sorting, live rows, the filter,

00:04:12.04 --> 00:04:15.05
the activity times and the early list request.

00:04:15.40 --> 00:04:18.70
All green, the backend has two hundred and two tests,

00:04:18.70 --> 00:04:23.86
the worker one hundred and forty, and the frontend one hundred and four unit tests.

00:04:23.86 --> 00:04:28.66
End to end, there are eleven new tests: the list, the filter across a reload,

00:04:28.66 --> 00:04:32.26
both empty states, a row updating live with a real worker,

00:04:32.26 --> 00:04:36.68
three visual comparisons with the mocks, and four accessibility checks.

00:04:36.68 --> 00:04:40.04
The two performance tests run on their own, in CI too.

00:04:41.19 --> 00:04:44.78
Here is the list, with projects in every state: running,

00:04:44.78 --> 00:04:48.95
paused at an Owner's request, blocked with the stage that failed,

00:04:48.95 --> 00:04:51.70
idle at one hundred percent, and cancelled.

00:04:51.70 --> 00:04:56.96
Each row shows the stage, what it is doing, how far along it is, what it has cost,

00:04:56.96 --> 00:04:58.94
and when it last did something.

00:04:59.29 --> 00:05:01.66
It works in dark mode and on a phone.

00:05:02.01 --> 00:05:03.00
Now the filter.

00:05:03.00 --> 00:05:06.69
The Owner chooses Blocked, and only Parcel Post is left,

00:05:06.69 --> 00:05:09.00
with the filter in the address bar.

00:05:09.00 --> 00:05:11.64
A reload, and the filter is still there.

00:05:11.64 --> 00:05:13.62
Then Running, and back to All.

00:05:13.97 --> 00:05:17.37
And here the list stays open while the workers run.

00:05:17.37 --> 00:05:20.72
Ledgerline and Tidewater move from stage to stage,

00:05:20.72 --> 00:05:25.53
their percentages climbing and their activity staying a few seconds old,

00:05:25.53 --> 00:05:26.93
all without a reload.

00:05:26.93 --> 00:05:31.48
Then an Owner submits Northwind Ops, and its row appears at the top.

00:05:31.83 --> 00:05:35.23
With no projects at all, an Owner sees New product,

00:05:35.23 --> 00:05:40.82
and a Viewer sees the same empty state, explaining that projects appear here once an

00:05:40.82 --> 00:05:42.75
Owner gives Hewn a direction.

00:05:43.10 --> 00:05:46.41
And here is the Lighthouse report for the production build.

00:05:47.56 --> 00:05:49.42
Journey eleven is complete.

00:05:49.42 --> 00:05:53.68
Projects lists every project with its stage, status, progress,

00:05:53.68 --> 00:05:56.30
spend and last activity, newest first.

00:05:56.30 --> 00:06:00.01
The filter lives in the address bar, rows change live,

00:06:00.01 --> 00:06:02.35
and the empty state fits the role.

00:06:02.35 --> 00:06:07.51
And the page meets its performance budgets, with the numbers checked in CI.

00:06:07.51 --> 00:06:09.30
Next comes journey twelve.
